### =====================================================================
### appointMe - day13-roles.http   (Day 13: roles and tenant scoping)
### SELF-CONTAINED: it logs in by itself and keeps its own tokens
### (ownerToken = Alice, OWNER of tenant 1; staffToken = Bob, STAFF in tenant 1),
### so it never touches the {{token}} used by your main requests.http.
###
### BEFORE YOU START: reload the seed so the data is known
###   Get-Content .\scripts\dev-seed.sql | docker exec -i appointme-db psql -U appointme -d appointme
###
### Run SETUP first, top to bottom. Tokens last 30 minutes.
### "EXPECT" is the status code you should see.
### =====================================================================

@baseUrl = http://localhost:8080
@ownerStaffId = 1
@bobStaffId = 2
@serviceId = 1
@customerId = 1


### ======================================================================
### SETUP
### ======================================================================

### O1. Alice logs in. EXPECT 200 (user token, no business yet)
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "alice@test.com", "password": "correct-horse-1" }

> {%
    client.global.set("ownerUserToken", response.body.accessToken);
%}

### O2. Alice selects tenant 1. EXPECT 200, role OWNER
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/select-tenant/1
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerUserToken}}

> {%
    client.global.set("ownerToken", response.body.accessToken);
%}

### W1. Bob logs in. EXPECT 200
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "bob@test.com", "password": "correct-horse-1" }

> {%
    client.global.set("staffUserToken", response.body.accessToken);
%}

### W2. Bob selects tenant 1. EXPECT 200, role STAFF
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/select-tenant/1
Authorization: Bearer {{staffUserToken}}

> {%
    client.global.set("staffToken", response.body.accessToken);
%}

### C0. Register Carol so the owner can add her as staff later. EXPECT 201 (rerun: 409)
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "carol@example.com", "password": "correct-horse-1", "fullName": "Carol Cutter" }


### ======================================================================
### 1. STAFF is NOT allowed to do owner-only things  (all EXPECT 403)
### ======================================================================

### P1. Staff creates a service. EXPECT 403 (NOT 500 - see the Day 13 trap)
POST {{baseUrl}}/services
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Staff Special", "durationMinutes": 10, "price": 5.00 }

### P2. Staff updates a service. EXPECT 403, and the service is unchanged
PUT {{baseUrl}}/services/{{serviceId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Hacked by staff", "durationMinutes": 1, "price": 0.00, "active": true }

### P3. Staff deletes a service. EXPECT 403, and active stays true
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/services/{{serviceId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}

### P4. Staff deletes a customer. EXPECT 403 (customer delete is owner-only)
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/customers/{{customerId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}

### P5. Staff adds a team member. EXPECT 403
POST {{baseUrl}}/staff
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "carol@example.com" }


### ======================================================================
### 2. STAFF is allowed to do day-to-day work  (EXPECT 200 / 201)
### ======================================================================

### P6. Staff lists services. EXPECT 200
GET {{baseUrl}}/services
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}

### P7. Staff creates a customer. EXPECT 201 (rerun: 409, same email)
POST {{baseUrl}}/customers
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "fullName": "Dana Walk-in", "email": "dana@example.com" }

### P8. Staff lists customers. EXPECT 200
GET {{baseUrl}}/customers
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}

### P9. Staff lists the team. EXPECT 200 (needed to pick a staffId when booking)
GET {{baseUrl}}/staff
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}


### ======================================================================
### 3. OWNER can do everything
### ======================================================================

### Q1. Owner creates a service. EXPECT 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/services
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Massage", "durationMinutes": 45, "price": 40.00 }

### Q2. Owner lists the team. EXPECT 200, Alice (OWNER) and Bob (STAFF), + Carol after Q3
GET {{baseUrl}}/staff
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}

### Q3. Owner adds Carol. EXPECT 201, role STAFF (rerun: 409)
POST {{baseUrl}}/staff
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "carol@example.com" }

### Q4. Add Carol again. EXPECT 409
POST {{baseUrl}}/staff
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "carol@example.com" }

### Q5. Add Alice herself (already a member). EXPECT 409
POST {{baseUrl}}/staff
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "alice@test.com" }

### Q6. Email nobody registered. EXPECT 404
POST {{baseUrl}}/staff
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "ghost@example.com" }

### Q7. Not an email. EXPECT 400
POST {{baseUrl}}/staff
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "nope" }

### Q8. Trying to smuggle a role in the body. EXPECT 409 if Carol exists already,
###     otherwise 201 - and either way the role is STAFF (the DTO has no role field)
POST {{baseUrl}}/staff
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "carol@example.com", "role": "OWNER" }


### ======================================================================
### 4. STAFF SEES ONLY THEIR OWN APPOINTMENTS
### (Alice = membership 1, Bob = membership 2; dates must be in the future)
### ======================================================================

### R1. Owner books an appointment for BOB. EXPECT 201 (stores its id)
POST {{baseUrl}}/appointments
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "staffId": {{bobStaffId}}, "customerId": {{customerId}}, "serviceId": {{serviceId}},
  "startsAt": "2026-11-17T09:00:00Z", "notes": "Bob's appointment" }

> {%
    client.global.set("bobApptId", response.body.id);
%}

### R2. Owner books an appointment for HERSELF. EXPECT 201 (stores its id)
POST {{baseUrl}}/appointments
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "staffId": {{ownerStaffId}}, "customerId": {{customerId}}, "serviceId": {{serviceId}},
  "startsAt": "2026-11-17T11:00:00Z", "notes": "Alice's appointment" }

> {%
    client.global.set("aliceApptId", response.body.id);
%}

### R3. Staff lists the range. EXPECT 200, ONLY Bob's appointment(s), never Alice's
GET {{baseUrl}}/appointments?from=2026-11-01T00:00:00Z&to=2026-12-01T00:00:00Z
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}

### R4. Owner lists the same range. EXPECT 200, BOTH appointments
GET {{baseUrl}}/appointments?from=2026-11-01T00:00:00Z&to=2026-12-01T00:00:00Z
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}

### R5. Staff reads Bob's own appointment. EXPECT 200
GET {{baseUrl}}/appointments/{{bobApptId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}

### R6. Staff reads ALICE's appointment. EXPECT 404 (same tenant, but not theirs)
GET {{baseUrl}}/appointments/{{aliceApptId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}

### R7. Staff changes status of ALICE's appointment. EXPECT 404, status unchanged
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/appointments/{{aliceApptId}}/status
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "NO_SHOW" }

### R8. Staff books on their OWN schedule. EXPECT 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/appointments
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "staffId": {{bobStaffId}}, "customerId": {{customerId}}, "serviceId": {{serviceId}},
  "startsAt": "2026-11-18T09:00:00Z" }

### R9. Staff books on ALICE's schedule. EXPECT 403 "own schedule"
POST {{baseUrl}}/appointments
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "staffId": {{ownerStaffId}}, "customerId": {{customerId}}, "serviceId": {{serviceId}},
  "startsAt": "2026-11-18T10:00:00Z" }

### R10. Staff completes their OWN appointment. EXPECT 200, COMPLETED
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/appointments/{{bobApptId}}/status
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "COMPLETED" }

### R11. Owner can change anyone's. EXPECT 200, NO_SHOW
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/appointments/{{aliceApptId}}/status
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "NO_SHOW" }


### ======================================================================
### 5. TOKEN-LEVEL CHECKS
### ======================================================================

### U1. USER token (no business selected) on an owner endpoint. EXPECT 403
POST {{baseUrl}}/services
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerUserToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Nope", "durationMinutes": 10, "price": 1.00 }

### U2. USER token on a read endpoint. EXPECT 403
GET {{baseUrl}}/services
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerUserToken}}

### U3. No token at all. EXPECT 401 (not 403: the caller is unknown)
POST {{baseUrl}}/staff
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "carol@example.com" }

### U4. FORGED ROLE. Take {{staffToken}}, run the Day 12 PowerShell snippet changing
###     "role":"STAFF" to "role":"OWNER", paste the forged token below. EXPECT 401
POST {{baseUrl}}/services
Authorization: Bearer PASTE.FORGED.TOKEN.HERE
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Forged", "durationMinutes": 10, "price": 1.00 }
